		This paper presents a recurrent neural network model governed by a set of
		strictly local, biologically motivated learning rules and architectural
		constraints. Unlike most existing models, all synaptic weights ---
		including inhibitory connections --- are learned through rules that
		depend only on information locally available at the synapse. Operating
		under geometric two-dimensional connectivity with a depth axis, Dale's
		law, and a timescale hierarchy of plasticity mechanisms grounded in
		control theory, the network achieves stable population-wide firing rate
		statistics without convergence to a fixed point. Individual neurons
		exhibit continuous representational drift while population statistics
		remain bounded --- consistent with recent experimental observations of
		representational drift in cortex. Feedforward weights spontaneously
		develop spatially localized, orientation-selective receptive fields
		without oriented structure in the input, while recurrent weights don't exhibit obvious geometric
		structuring. Synaptic weight distributions emerge as lognormal across
		all synapse types. The model exhibits broad robustness to initialization
		across a wide range of L1 norm values. We discuss the biological
		interpretation of the learning rules, their relationship to synaptic
		scaling, metabolic constraints, and silent synapse dynamics, and
		identify potential links to neuromorphic implementations.
		
	\end{abstract}
	
