	\section{Introduction}
	
	% Suggested flow:
	% - The problem: stable recurrent learning under biological constraints
	% - What is typically assumed/cheated in existing models
	% - Your approach: take constraints seriously
	% - Brief summary of results
	% - Roadmap of paper
	
	The neocortex solves a remarkable engineering problem: it learns
	continuously, maintains stable representations, and reorganizes itself
	--- all using only local information at each synapse, with no access to
	global error signals or centralized coordination. Reproducing this
	capacity in computational models has proven difficult. Most existing
	approaches either relax the constraint of locality
	\citep{placeholder1}, fix inhibitory weights
	\citep{placeholder2}, require careful initialization
	\citep{placeholder3}, or impose explicit convergence mechanisms
	that have no clear biological counterpart \citep{placeholder4}.
	
	Recent experimental work has complicated the picture further by
	demonstrating that neural representations in cortex drift continuously
	even in stable environments --- a phenomenon termed representational
	drift \citep{placeholder5}. This suggests that convergence to a fixed
	point is not the goal of biological learning, and that stability is a
	statistical property of the population rather than of individual
	neurons.
	
	Here we present a model that takes biological constraints seriously at
	every level of design. We impose strict locality throughout: no synapse
	has access to information beyond its own weight, the firing rates of
	its pre- and postsynaptic neurons, and local statistics derived from
	these. We enforce Dale's law, geometric connectivity, and a timescale
	hierarchy of plasticity mechanisms motivated by the biological
	literature. We learn inhibitory weights using the same local framework
	as excitatory weights.
	
	Under these constraints, we find that the network exhibits stable
	population firing rate statistics without individual convergence,
	spontaneous development of spatially organized feedforward receptive
	fields, orientation-like selectivity without oriented input, lognormal
	weight distributions, and broad robustness to initialization. We argue
	that these properties emerge as natural consequences of the constraints
	rather than as designed outcomes.
	
	The paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:model} describes
	the network architecture. Section~\ref{sec:learning} presents the
	learning rules and their biological motivation. Section~\ref{sec:results}
	reports the results. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses the
	relationship to biology, existing models, and potential applications.
	
